// library inludes - backslash = a line that starts at any point and goes down and slightly to the left but stays straight none the less and stops after about an 1/8 of an inch. Same definition for the second backslash. A lower case l is a line that starts at any point and goes exactly straight down for about an 1/8 of an inch. A lower case i is a short line starting at any point and going straight down for a 1/16 of an inch with a dot above it almost like a little sun on top of a little bare tree. A b is a line that starts at any point and goes straight down for an 1/8 of an inch, it also has another curved line attached to it that starts about half way down the original line and goes to the right and swoops down and back to left and connects to the bottom of the original line. A lower case r is a little line that starts at any point and goes down about a 1/16 of an inch and also has another line connected starting slightly below where the original line started and goes to the right slightly arching up and then back down then stops almost looking like the top half of a sun. A lower case a is line more complicated curved line that starts at any point and goes to right about a 1/16 of an inch an curves down to the right and then goes straight down and stops after about a 1/16 of an inch, there's another line that starts just barely below where the first line started except it's connected below the curve on the straight part of the original line and goes left and curves down for a 1/16 of an inch goes back to the right and connects to the bottom of the original line. See previous definition for lower case r. A lower case y is a line that starts at any point and goes down and slightly to the left for an 1/8 of an inch, it also has another line attached to it starting half way down the original line and goes up and slightly to the left so the top should look like a v. See previous definition for lower case i.  
